#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069582} :activity/id #uuid "49e747aa-67fa-5a5d-956a-efff2f77cce1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363 :topic/id #uuid "98de9504-2d24-511e-8d7b-74400569101d" :topic/math-academy-id 477} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070005 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph of $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99cee7824a157e5a08292ebba9e60a5633c7a2f8a5ebb496fc1d5dbe14f858e3.png)\n\nWe can *approximate* the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1$.\n\n![](images/8a/8aa7a3f9be8371b1459874cd2cb8c0c4caf1000d76b99d134fb6e806e638c548.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(1)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(2)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle) + [f(3)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(width)]^(⏞)^([math]3[/math]rd rectangle); = [1^{2} \\cdot 1] + [2^{2} \\cdot 1] + [3^{2} \\cdot 1]; = 1 + 4 + 9; = 14\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **left Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*left* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4]$, the rectangles are entirely *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." :tutorial/id #uuid "196ab182-655a-56ac-9e26-89a3d2835dde" :tutorial/math-academy-id 727 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a126e5fd-355d-55ad-b4aa-e14b49d64d66" :step/math-academy-id 1400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068553 :question/id #uuid "f781ef9b-491f-5df4-a273-94f30ce79185" :question/math-academy-id "e-389"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235475}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "30e05a77-f052-5774-b6ca-d2d8db22d397" :step/math-academy-id 1401 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070006 :tutorial/content "In general, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ can be approximated using a left Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/85/8547bd96f866100db3ed28f3f23e3446ab5eff50632c425c23c2916c89f823db.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(x_0)\\Delta x+f(x_1)\\Delta x+f(x_2)\\Delta x+ \\cdots + f(x_{n-1}) \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the $\\Delta x$, which gives the following general formula:\n\n$$\nheights\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e2ae388a-a2b0-584b-bf06-7b66f82ce253" :tutorial/math-academy-id 729 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Left Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "fc16ac0f-77ae-504b-9997-be48eb6a1191" :step/math-academy-id 1404 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069583}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070007 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta x$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a left Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nFirst, note that the domain of $f(x)$ has been *partitioned* as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} [1,4] &= [1, 2] \\cup [2, 3.5] \\cup [3.5, 4]. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/2e/2e8563480f46ff335042c3c52a6f60983f871dccb886c8cf3ca0844504a73db6.png)\n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our left Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the left endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{A} &\\approx {\\color{red}1} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2-1}) + {\\color{red}4} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}3.5-2}) + {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-3.5}) \\\\[5pt] &= {\\color{red}1} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1} +{\\color{red}4} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5}+ {\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} \\\\[5pt] &=1+6+3 \\\\[5pt] &= 10. \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "86c2c7f1-e8cd-59f5-a5ed-1be84ec0a424" :tutorial/math-academy-id 730 :tutorial/title "Left Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "b3afc098-fc93-5ec7-9554-3989f6fd68d6" :step/math-academy-id 1405 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068704 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068554 :question/id #uuid "6220f2e1-e654-5a30-a0f8-377bb0d64bc7" :question/math-academy-id "e-390"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00d82333-e4e0-5527-a084-0af8affe5bee" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373153}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "bd0626fb-e960-598c-8d94-fb531e7ccce6" :step/math-academy-id 1406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068640 :question/id #uuid "797ec111-8928-5875-8f94-9a423ff558f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-7228"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069374}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "c489e48f-0e35-581c-85cc-0974d9100b36" :step/math-academy-id 26738 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069722}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068641 :question/id #uuid "60eae905-fa87-5eac-bff2-8326ae41ca1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7230"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069376}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"} :step/id #uuid "c4ba3e71-564e-5615-b646-afbf5d8127c9" :step/math-academy-id 26741 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069981 :tutorial/content "Whether the left Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.![](images/88/883b1d5faacf246557319e1f2b4fa253a06f657c589b24e5ec27eb35c00528c5.png)\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the left Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/ab/ab23c5b0d95a6bd78ac337266bb504c63b528d756548cbcd38e8b236ef140bc3.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe559575-cc25-5a45-a1b2-fa3254bed0b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4372 :tutorial/title "Overestimates and Underestimates"} :step/id #uuid "02dd1ff6-87f8-57cd-b528-ab8c67dc87b7" :step/math-academy-id 26748 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069721}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas With the Left Riemann Sum" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186069000}]}
